That Sunday morning, you are not making “trance” in the normal sense.

You are making something like Dark Ritual Minimal / Polyrhythmic Analog Techno with Melodic Tension.

More specifically, that exact setup pushes you toward:

DFAM → twitchy percussion, unstable metallic hits, little bursts of violence
Mother-32 → bassline / sequence / utility voice
Matriarch → big analog phrases, drones, pressure, harmonic gravity
Subharmonicon → polyrhythmic clock weirdness, non-4/4 movement hiding inside 4/4
Analog Rytm MKII → the actual body: kick, toms, drive, physical groove
Digitone II → cold digital chords, metallic FM percussion, glassy counter-rhythm
Analog Heat MKII → turns the whole thing from “nice modular jam” into “something is happening in the basement.”

So the result would probably live somewhere between:

hypnotic techno
dark ambient techno
Berlin-school sequencing gone feral
minimal industrial
polyrhythmic electro-acoustic machine music

But with your musical instincts, I don't think you'd leave it purely abstract.

You would probably sneak in one beautiful Matriarch line or Digitone chord progression and suddenly the whole thing becomes:
dark, mechanized, slightly threatening, but emotionally open

Which is basically your recurring design language.

The Sunday-morning version would be slower than Dark Floor, maybe 112–124 BPM, more spacious, less “club weapon,” more “predawn laboratory with coffee.”

I can hear an arrangement like this:

Rytm holds a very simple heavy kick.
DFAM makes irregular secondary percussion.
Subharmonicon provides the strange internal pulse.
Mother-32 runs a restrained bass sequence.
Digitone II adds short icy chords every few bars.
Matriarch waits.

Then after six minutes the Matriarch finally enters with one enormous, slow phrase.

That is the moment where the piece stops being modular noodling and becomes music.

And Heat MKII at the end of the chain gives it cohesion and a little danger.

If you want a name for the style, I'd call it:
Dark Sunday Machine Music
or, in Fleet 2 language:
Dark Morning — Modular Edition
Not panic. Not pursuit.

Just all the mothers awake before sunrise, arguing with the Elektrons.
